libpcap: capture on Linux with recvmsg() where there is no mmap()

libpcap 1.10 reads packets only from the memory-mapped ring (TPACKET_V2/V3).
The guest is WebAssembly: a process's memory is its own linear memory, which
cannot map the kernel's ring, and the guest's libc has no mmap() at all. With
PCAP_LINUX_NO_MMAP (set on __wasm__), libpcap reads one packet at a time with
recvmsg(), as it did before 1.10:

- setup_recv() replaces setup_mmapped(): a buffer laid out as a ring frame
  (a tpacket2_hdr's room, the sockaddr_ll, headroom for a cooked header and a
  VLAN tag, the packet), PACKET_AUXDATA (VLAN tag, length on the wire),
  SO_TIMESTAMPNS (arrival time), and a 2 MB receive buffer standing in for the
  ring (SO_RCVBUFFORCE, or SO_RCVBUF as far as rmem_max lets it).
- pcap_read_linux_recv() receives into that buffer and hands each packet to
  pcap_handle_packet_mmap(), which does what it does for the ring: cooked
  headers, VLAN tags, direction, userland filtering, snapshot length.
- The wait (pcap_wait_for_frames_mmap) returns when the timeout goes by, so
  pcap_dispatch() with a timeout returns, as it did with recvfrom().
- pcap_setfilter_linux(): no ring blocks to filter in userland after a new
  kernel filter; set_kernel_filter() drains the socket as before.
- pcap_lib_version() says "with recvmsg(), without memory-mapped capture"; a
  kernel without PF_PACKET gets "the kernel needs CONFIG_PACKET".

The ring code stays (mmap()/munmap() are stubs that fail) and is not reached.

